## Runtime and platform baseline

| Technology | Responsibility | Engineering implication |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| TypeScript | Static checking before execution | Enable strict checking; validate external values at runtime. |
| Hermes | JavaScript execution engine | TypeScript is transformed to JavaScript; Hermes and JSI are different components. |
| JSI | Interface between JavaScript and native code | Native access may be synchronous; large synchronous work can still block the calling JS thread. |
| Fabric | React Native rendering infrastructure | Verify native component compatibility in the application's supported build configuration. |
| TurboModules | Native module infrastructure | Check each native dependency's integration requirements; not every native package is implemented identically. |
| Redux Toolkit | Typed reducers, store configuration, and asynchronous workflows | Keep state serializable and isolate effects from reducers. |
| MMKV | Local key-value persistence | Validate serialized records; use approved encryption and account lifecycle policies. |

Use the versions pinned in the approved project template and lockfile. Native dependency compatibility includes React Native, React, the JS engine, Android/iOS toolchains, and any required native integration packages. A JavaScript type check does not replace native release builds.

**Example API baseline:** Redux Toolkit 2.x, React Redux 9.1+ typed hooks, Zod 4 schemas, and an MMKV v4 adapter. MMKV v4 uses `createMMKV` and Nitro Modules; do not mix its setup with older `new MMKV` or `delete` examples. Follow the documentation for the version actually installed in your application.

See the [React Native architecture overview](https://reactnative.dev/architecture/overview) and [MMKV version-specific documentation](https://github.com/margelo/react-native-mmkv).

## State ownership

Use one owner for each kind of state. Redux Toolkit does not mean every temporary UI value must live in Redux.

| State | Preferred owner |
| :--- | :--- |
| Input text, a local toggle, or temporary component interaction | Local React state unless another part of the app needs it |
| Shared workflow state or cross-screen product state | A Redux Toolkit slice |
| Server data with cache invalidation, shared subscriptions, and deduplication | RTK Query when it is the project's data-fetching standard |
| A multi-step operation coordinating local cache and remote refresh | A typed thunk or established workflow layer |
| Data retained across app restarts | A validated persistence adapter with explicit schema and retention policy |
| Credentials and session secrets | The approved authentication and secure-storage SDK, outside ordinary Redux state and profile cache |

**This example uses `createAsyncThunk`** to make the cache-read → remote-refresh → persistence sequence explicit. Redux Toolkit provides the store, slice, lifecycle actions, request IDs, and abort signal. It does not introduce Zustand or TanStack Query.

RTK Query is included in Redux Toolkit and is the Redux team's recommended purpose-built solution for ordinary server-data fetching and caching. If the application already uses RTK Query, adapt the repository/use-case boundary to that API layer instead of keeping a second copy of the same server data in a profile slice. Establish one policy for in-memory caching, durable storage, refresh, and invalidation. See the [RTK Query overview](https://redux.js.org/toolkit/rtk-query/overview).

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · loading and content can coexist">
A cached profile can remain visible while a refresh is running. Represent content, refresh progress, and failure messaging independently. “User exists while loading” is a useful refresh state, not an impossible state.
</Aside>

## Feature structure and boundaries

```text
src/
├── app/
│   ├── store.ts                         # Root store, reducer registration, thunk dependencies
│   ├── hooks.ts                         # Typed dispatch and selector hooks
│   └── SessionRoot.tsx                  # Minimal account-session Provider example
└── features/user/
    ├── domain/
    │   └── profile.ts                   # Domain model, errors, repository contract, use case
    ├── data/
    │   ├── remote/userApiService.ts     # HTTP boundary and DTO validation
    │   ├── local/userStorage.ts         # Account-scoped, versioned cache adapter
    │   ├── local/mmkvAdapter.ts         # Injected encrypted MMKV v4 instance
    │   └── repository/userRepository.ts # Mapping and persistence coordination
    ├── di/
    │   └── createProfileUseCase.ts      # Dependency construction
    └── presentation/
        ├── state/profileTypes.ts       # Serializable screen state and failure contracts
        ├── state/profileSlice.ts       # Typed thunk, slice, request guards, selectors
        ├── hooks/useUserProfile.ts      # Screen lifecycle and cancellation
        └── views/UserProfileRoute.tsx  # Route adapter and state-rendering view
```

| Boundary | Rule |
| :--- | :--- |
| Presentation → domain | Call use cases. Do not import HTTP clients or storage instances into views. |
| Domain → data | Depend on an interface; the data layer implements it. |
| API/cache → domain | Treat external values as `unknown`, validate them, then map to domain values. |
| Effects → Redux | Dispatch serializable data and failure categories. Do not dispatch promises, SDK clients, or abort controllers as state. |
| Composition → dependencies | Inject account-scoped implementations through thunk middleware, outside Redux state. |

Feature directories make ownership visible. Enforce import boundaries with the project's lint rules or package boundaries; directory names alone do not enforce architecture. Reusable application-wide UI and infrastructure modules remain appropriate when they have clear owners.

