This is a minimal root-store example. Merge the reducer and dependency injection into the real application store; do not add a nested Redux store for each feature. The default serializability and immutability checks remain enabled.

### `src/app/store.ts`

```typescript
import { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';
import { profilesReducer } from '../features/user/presentation/state/profileSlice';
import type { ProfileDependencies } from '../features/user/presentation/state/profileTypes';

/** Minimal reference store. Integrate this reducer into the real root store. */
export function makeSessionStore(dependencies: ProfileDependencies) {
  return configureStore({
    reducer: { profiles: profilesReducer },
    middleware: (getDefaultMiddleware) => getDefaultMiddleware({
      thunk: { extraArgument: dependencies },
    }),
    devTools: false, // Enable deliberately in approved development builds only.
  });
}
export type AppStore = ReturnType<typeof makeSessionStore>;
export type RootState = ReturnType<AppStore['getState']>;
export type AppDispatch = AppStore['dispatch'];
```

### `src/app/hooks.ts`

```typescript
import { useDispatch, useSelector } from 'react-redux';
import type { AppDispatch, RootState } from './store';

// React Redux 9.1+ typed hooks; use your project's approved compatible version.
export const useAppDispatch = useDispatch.withTypes<AppDispatch>();
export const useAppSelector = useSelector.withTypes<RootState>();
```

<Aside type="tip" title="Hint · dependencies belong in middleware, not state">
Thunk `extraArgument` carries the use case and diagnostics adapter. They are not serialized into the store or passed in a dispatched action. Infer store and dispatch types from the configured store, then use the pretyped hooks throughout presentation code.
</Aside>

For this reference, the store and injected dependencies belong to one authenticated session. Recreating only the use case variable does not replace the store's existing middleware dependency.

### `src/app/SessionRoot.tsx`

```tsx
import { useState, type PropsWithChildren } from 'react';
import { Provider } from 'react-redux';
import type { ProfileDependencies } from '../features/user/presentation/state/profileTypes';
import { makeSessionStore } from './store';

type SessionProps = PropsWithChildren<{
  sessionScopeKey: string;
  dependencies: ProfileDependencies;
}>;

/** Minimal app-root example. Do not mount a second Redux Provider inside a feature. */
export function SessionRoot({ sessionScopeKey, dependencies, children }: SessionProps) {
  return (
    <SessionProvider key={sessionScopeKey} dependencies={dependencies}>
      {children}
    </SessionProvider>
  );
}

function SessionProvider({ dependencies, children }: PropsWithChildren<{
  dependencies: ProfileDependencies;
}>) {
  const [store] = useState(() => makeSessionStore(dependencies));
  return <Provider store={store}>{children}</Provider>;
}
```

`sessionScopeKey` is a non-secret identity that changes for each authenticated session. A new key remounts the session subtree and creates a store with the new dependencies. This component illustrates ownership at the app root; integrate it with the existing application shell and all existing reducers. Do not mount it inside an individual profile screen.

For an application that retains one root store across login/logout, adapt the dependency resolver and session-reset workflow deliberately. Reset every account-scoped slice, cancel in-flight work, and prevent old-session results from rehydrating protected state.

