### Review checklist

- [ ] Redux Toolkit and typed React Redux hooks follow the application's established setup.
- [ ] Framework and native dependency versions match the project lockfile and supported toolchain.
- [ ] Domain code imports no React, Redux, React Native, MMKV, or HTTP implementation.
- [ ] Network payloads and stored JSON are validated at runtime before use.
- [ ] Each screen instance has a unique state entry and request-ID guards.
- [ ] Request cancellation reaches the HTTP adapter and is checked before local writes.
- [ ] Refresh progress and failures can coexist with valid cached content.
- [ ] Invalid credentials/access never become an offline success response.
- [ ] Credentials, abort controllers, SDK objects, and functions are absent from state and dispatched payloads.
- [ ] Managed encryption keys are supplied at runtime and never hardcoded.
- [ ] Account switching resets protected state and persistent data according to policy.
- [ ] Reducers are pure; Immer draft updates occur only in Redux Toolkit reducers.
- [ ] Diagnostics omit payloads, credentials, raw exception strings, and account identifiers.
- [ ] React Native focus, backgrounding, unmount, and Strict Mode lifecycle behavior are exercised.
- [ ] Android and iOS release builds, accessibility checks, and required CI gates pass.

Use the coverage threshold configured in the repository. The supplied draft contained conflicting percentages; this page does not invent a replacement policy.

### Essential test scenarios

Test validation, schema corruption, account isolation, API status mapping, persistence failure, cached-error state, access revocation, cleanup failure, cancellation before write, late response rejection, separate screen entries, state reset, and cache-read recovery. Use deferred promises or controlled test doubles to establish ordering rather than timing-sensitive sleeps.

